-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': "**Hand Analysis: A Potentially Tricky Spot**\n\nOkay, so I'm in hand number 7, with pocket Queens – Qh, Qd – a decent starting hand. I'm up in chips overall, which is good, but I've already bet 1000 so far this hand. The board is looking nasty though: 3c, 7d, Ad, 4c. Ace-high, ugh. My opponent, who seems to be acting first, checked the turn. So far, the betting history looks like: I raised pre-flop, they called, I bet the flop, they called again. Now, the turn card.\n\nHere's the problem: that Ace on the board. My Queens are now, at best, second pair. They're vulnerable to any Ace, of course, but also possibly flush draws if they're holding diamonds. The 4c on the turn doesn't really change the situation. I'm trying to figure out what my opponent might have. They called my pre-flop raise and flop bet, which suggests they've connected with the board somehow – maybe an Ace (Ax), a flush draw, or a pair. They could also be floating, but that seems less likely on an Ace-high board. A check on the turn is pretty standard for marginal hands or draws.\n\nI'm in position, which is good. But this Ace on the board is a real scare card. If I bet again, I'm likely just getting value from hands that are better than mine (Ax, sets, two pair) or getting called by draws (diamond flushes). If I check, I can potentially induce a bluff on the river or get to showdown cheaply if the board pairs and they were on a draw. The pot is already a decent size, and I don't want to make it bigger when I'm probably behind. It's a pretty clear pot control situation. Ultimately, I'm checking here, hoping to see a cheap river and get to showdown.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: I hold Pocket Queens, which is a strong starting hand. However, the community cards include an Ace (Ace of Diamonds), which significantly devalues my hand as any opponent holding an Ace currently beats me. The opponent called my pre-flop raise and my bet on the Flop. This suggests they likely have a piece of the board (possibly an Ace, a pair of 7s, or a draw). Betting again on the Turn has "negative implied odds": worse hands (like 88-JJ or 7x) will likely fold, while better hands (Ax, two pair, sets) will call or raise. By checking, I exercise pot control, avoid inflating the pot with a marginal hand relative to the board, and look to reach a cheaper showdown.\n\nReason: There is an Ace on the board, and the opponent has called previous bets, indicating they may have an Ace or a strong draw. Betting folds out worse hands and keeps better hands in. Checking allows me to see the River for free, controlling the pot size while retaining bluff-catching potential.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
